notes:
  - "One pre-existing test was deliberately INVERTED, and it is the most load-bearing change in the WU. closeConfirm.test.tsx asserted 'a revert that fails still quits (never traps the user)', satisfied by `remove: () => invoke('unset_proxy').catch(() => {}).then(quit)` — a failed unset quit anyway, stranding the machine on a dead proxy with no internet, precisely the outcome the dialog exists to prevent. The invariant was right and its implementation was wrong: the user must not be trapped, but quitting is not how you avoid trapping them. `remove` now propagates the rejection, and the review then found a second layer to it — only the unset may report failure, because once the proxy IS off a window that will not close is not 'couldn't turn the proxy off', and telling the user to undo it by hand would be advice to undo nothing."
  - "StepStatusView.message was removed entirely, not merely left unread. Adding `short` made the full sentence dead payload — no component or screen read `.message` — and 16 duplicated sentences pinned by a test is a second source of truth for wording the design had already moved. The field is gone and the resolvers return a verdict plus an optional remedy."
  - "The design specifies StatusLine's toned message; TonedText was the obvious vehicle and is the wrong one — it renders font-mono, while StatusLine's anatomy requires Inter. The tone is applied with a local three-key lookup and TonedText still owns the glyph, so the second channel is intact."
  - "CopyText swallowed a rejected clipboard write while its sibling CopyButton flashed 'error' through the same shared hook, so adopting it would have preserved exactly the silence this WU was assigned to remove. Note the narrower true statement, since the first version of this note overstated it: FlowDetail.tsx:58 still swallows its rejection. That screen is not this WU's and is untouched by the diff — it is W6's surface — but the sentence 'no unguarded clipboard call remains under ui/src' was false as written and is not repeated."
